 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
+### §check-fails-closed — any internal failure aborts the check with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-22
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. A failing command ends the check with a non-zero exit instead of being swallowed.
+- Forbidden: removing `-e` (or `errexit`), or masking failures with `|| true`, `2>/dev/null` or an unconditional
+  `exit 0` on an error path.
+- Reason: maintainer decision (2026-10-22). Under fail-open, breakage inside the check went unnoticed. Supersedes
+  §check-fails-open. The 2026-09-10 trigger (a staged-then-deleted file) is handled by the `[ -f "$f" ]` guard.
+  Under `-e`, a conditional must be written as `if …; then …; fi`, not `[ … ] && …`, because a false test as the
+  loop's last command fails the pipeline.
+
+### §check-fails-open — superseded 2026-10-22 by §check-fails-closed
 - Since: 2026-09-11
 - Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
 - Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
